# The fusions that owe no exact composed remainder. One row per (composition, composed layer) whose
# demand or nameplate sum is suspended, at the layer itself or at a layer its remainder walk passes
# through; or which, at the layer itself or at a layer its walk reaches, has no demand or nameplate
# figure.
#
# Kept to two quantities, and that is the argument
#   The remainder depends on exactly two of the three quantities a sum can be suspended in. A
#   suspended draw says nothing about the remainder and must not suspend it; a suspended demand or
#   nameplate suspends it entirely, because the correction for the eliminations then has a term
#   nobody sized.
#
# Either quantity, not the row as a whole
#   So this drops `quantity`, and the claim is that either of the two is suspended, not that the
#   row as a whole is. The source is keyed (composition, composed_layer, quantity) and this is keyed
#   without it, which is exactly the shape to watch: keeping matching rows and dropping a column
#   looks the same as grouping, and the two claim opposite things. Read the second way, a suspended
#   draw would veto a remainder that can be worked out perfectly well.
#
# A NULL `quantity` suspends every quantity
#   A NULL `quantity` is the suspension of the whole list, and here suspension's two levels arrive
#   in the filter itself. `eliminations/unsized` names the item and carries a quantity;
#   `eliminations/unsearched` names the list (nobody looked for double counting at all) and has no
#   quantity, so its rows come through with `quantity` NULL. An `IN` list does not match NULL, so
#   keeping to two values alone would quietly drop the suspension that is broader than either of
#   them. The arithmetic catches that where no law would, as the probe below shows.
#
# Below the layer as well as at it
#   Where a part's conversion is a range, composition/remainder_frontier.sqlc walks past that part
#   into its own parts, and the corrections that layer's composer applied come in through
#   composition/carried_eliminations.sqlc. If that layer's own demand or nameplate sum owes nothing,
#   because nobody searched or the overlap was found and not sized, the carried correction is not a
#   number, so the remainder above it is suspended too. The layers walked through join the layer
#   itself as the places a suspension is looked for.
#
# And wherever a layer it reaches has no figure to read
#   The remainder worked out from the parts adds up each stopping layer's own remainder, and a layer
#   with no demand or nameplate figure has none, so it would drop out of the sum and leave it short
#   with nothing failing. That happens where a layer files one of them `derived` and its computation
#   cannot finish, and at any stopping layer that files neither;
#   composition/resolved_quantities.sqlc says which. Every such layer suspends the remainder above
#   it.
#
# And at the layer itself
#   A composed layer whose own demand or nameplate has no figure has no remainder from its totals
#   for the one from its parts to be held against, so its remainder is suspended too, by the same
#   test on the layer. One that files the figure `derived` and has it computed is read at that
#   figure (layers/demand.sqlc, layers/nameplate.sqlc) and owes its remainder like any other.
#
# The filed grounds, not the whole set
#   The grounds read here are composition/filed_grounds.sqlc, which is every ground but
#   composition/underived_parts.sqlc. That one suspends a sum over a part filed `derived` whose
#   figure cannot be computed, and the walk reaches every part of a layer it passes and finds no
#   computed figure there, which the branch above catches. That branch reads
#   composition/resolved_quantities.sqlc, so the derived walk is composed here once already;
#   reading the ground as well would add no row and compose the walk a second time beneath every
#   rule that reads a remainder.
#
# Probe
#   examples/probes runs it as `owed_remainder`. Drop the `s.quantity IS NULL` test: the rest of
#   this relation must then owe more fusions than have their demand answered (owed as a sum, or
#   filed `derived`), which is impossible, because a condition needing both quantities answered
#   cannot cover more fusions than one needing the demand alone.
-- composition/filed_grounds.sqlc kept to the two quantities the remainder is built from, at the
-- layer or a node its walk passes; composition/resolved_quantities.sqlc without a demand or
-- nameplate figure at the layer or a node the walk reaches.
SELECT DISTINCT t.root_filing AS composition, t.root_layer AS composed_layer
FROM      (
    SELECT f.filing AS root_filing, f.layer AS root_layer, f.filing, f.layer
    FROM (
        :compose(composition/fusions.sqlc)
    ) f
    UNION ALL
    SELECT w.root_filing, w.root_layer, w.filing, w.layer
    FROM (
        :compose(composition/passed_nodes.sqlc)
    ) w
) t
JOIN      (
    :compose(composition/filed_grounds.sqlc)
) s ON s.composition = t.filing AND s.composed_layer = t.layer
WHERE s.quantity IS NULL
   OR s.quantity IN ('demand', 'nameplate')
UNION
SELECT w.root_filing, w.root_layer
FROM      (
    SELECT f.filing AS root_filing, f.layer AS root_layer, f.filing, f.layer
    FROM (
        :compose(composition/fusions.sqlc)
    ) f
    UNION ALL
    SELECT r.root_filing, r.root_layer, r.filing, r.layer
    FROM (
        :compose(composition/remainder_frontier.sqlc)
    ) r
) w
JOIN      (
    :compose(composition/resolved_quantities.sqlc)
) q ON q.filing = w.filing AND q.layer = w.layer
WHERE q.quantity IN ('demand', 'nameplate')
  AND q.low IS NULL
